**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 14 in full; [Química Geral I — Feitosa, Barbosa, and Forte](../sources/Quimica-Geral-I-UECE.lingua#r_7N9JXVMC5DWB8P3MX79YYPHNPA), the complete acid/base and nomenclature chapter; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), Understanding acids and bases and the first advanced acid/base lessons; [Principles of Chemical Science — MIT 5.112, Fall 2005](../sources/MIT-5-112.lingua#r_05VDAXGTY0M9E5K9TMJTWTFNAG), lectures 19–20.

Brønsted–Lowry acid/base descriptions track proton transfer and conjugate pairs. Lewis descriptions track electron-pair acceptance/donation and support a broader class of interactions. State the solvent, species, and reaction when identifying an acid or base. A substance's behavior can depend on the partner and conditions rather than one permanent label.

Distinguish strength from concentration and extent of dissociation from total amount. A concentrated weak acid and a dilute strong acid can have different analytical amounts and measured pH; neither adjective supplies the other's information. Conjugate relations and solvent leveling help explain which comparisons are meaningful in a chosen medium.

Water can act in more than one acid/base role and participates in autoprotolysis. The simple aqueous model introduces hydronium/proton notation and hydroxide with an equilibrium relation; the microscopic solvation picture is richer than one isolated free proton. Use the notation consistently while recognizing its level of description.

**Work:** identify conjugate pairs, write proton-transfer equations, and analyze a Lewis example from the book. Compare strength and concentration with explicit hypothetical amounts and equilibrium assumptions. Explain how changing solvent can alter the comparison without changing the formal definition of a conjugate pair.

Read the complete acid/base chapter and Portuguese theory/naming treatment. Then connect the qualitative account with the balances and activities already developed. The same principles support biology, geochemistry, corrosion, processing, and medicine, while each will supply its own chemical systems and evidence.

